WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.229 --> 00:00:04.950
Let's translate various film terminology and best practices

2
00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:07.830
into those applicable to game development.

3
00:00:07.830 --> 00:00:10.869
On the left is a list of various actors, components,

4
00:00:10.869 --> 00:00:13.830
and things Unreal Engine includes.

5
00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:16.829
It's important to note that many of the items can be added to

6
00:00:16.829 --> 00:00:19.309
and manipulated within a sequence.

7
00:00:19.309 --> 00:00:21.289
Control Rig, for an example, is predominantly

8
00:00:21.289 --> 00:00:23.510
animated through Sequencer.

9
00:00:23.510 --> 00:00:25.589
On the right are various terminologies

10
00:00:25.589 --> 00:00:28.230
we use when working with sequences.

11
00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:31.429
Scene Assemblies are the complete encapsulated collection

12
00:00:31.429 --> 00:00:36.710
of actors, animations, and logic nested within a sequence.

13
00:00:36.710 --> 00:00:38.090
Performance Takes is a feature that

14
00:00:38.090 --> 00:00:40.950
allows you to record animation from motion capture

15
00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:45.229
performances or input devices using an iterative workflow

16
00:00:45.229 --> 00:00:48.509
to automatically record the motion to a sequence.

17
00:00:48.509 --> 00:00:51.109
This tool is extremely useful time saver

18
00:00:51.109 --> 00:00:54.549
in capturing multiple variations of a single shot

19
00:00:54.549 --> 00:00:57.170
and can be reviewed on the fly.

20
00:00:57.170 --> 00:00:59.729
Takes can be swapped directly within a sequence

21
00:00:59.729 --> 00:01:03.950
via the shot properties from the dropdown menu.

22
00:01:03.950 --> 00:01:06.189
Sets, layout, staging, these all

23
00:01:06.189 --> 00:01:09.670
refer to the assembly of our actors in our scene

24
00:01:09.670 --> 00:01:13.269
or within our sequence via spawnables or swappables

25
00:01:13.269 --> 00:01:15.870
that essentially create the final look of our scene.

26
00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:17.709
Level Sequenced with Shots refers

27
00:01:17.709 --> 00:01:20.629
to our graduated or parent sequences

28
00:01:20.629 --> 00:01:23.540
within nested sequences or shots.

29
00:01:23.540 --> 00:01:25.939
It's important to note that shots are not

30
00:01:25.939 --> 00:01:27.939
a specific actor type.

31
00:01:27.939 --> 00:01:30.980
In fact, subtracts, shots, nested sequences,

32
00:01:30.980 --> 00:01:33.980
and parent tracks are all the same actor type.

33
00:01:33.980 --> 00:01:35.420
They're all sequences.

34
00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:38.019
It's how you use those sequences that really

35
00:01:38.019 --> 00:01:40.180
determines its functionality.

36
00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:42.700
On the left are various stages or assemblies

37
00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:45.719
when working with traditional film practices.

38
00:01:45.719 --> 00:01:47.939
On the right, we've translated those terminologies

39
00:01:47.939 --> 00:01:51.500
into terminology commonly used with Unreal Engine,

40
00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:54.140
specifically when working with sequences.

41
00:01:54.140 --> 00:01:56.340
I won't cover each line, but I do

42
00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:58.340
encourage you to pause the video

43
00:01:58.340 --> 00:02:01.579
and read through the slide to get more in-depth information

44
00:02:01.579 --> 00:02:05.180
and understanding how we translate film terminology

45
00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:07.780
that we use in Unreal.
